= Sesame Seeds

_Scope: This entry treats sesame —_ Sesamum indicum _L. (Pedaliaceae) — and its principal preparations: the_ raw seed _(white / black / brown / red cultivars),_ sesame oil _(_tila-taila_ / _simsim_ oil / 麻油),_ sesame paste _(_ṭaḥīna_ / tahini),_ sesame meal _, and the regional sweets (Indian_ til-laḍḍū_, Levantine_ halawa_, Chinese_ heizhima zhou_). Sesame is one of the most pan-classically attested foods in the materia medica — classical in all four canonical traditions and substantially the same drug in each, with deep ritual register in Hindu ancestor rites (_tarpaṇa _/_ piṇḍa-dāna_) and a striking_ longevity-and-hair-blackening _convergence across Ayurveda, Chinese medicine, and Tibb (and modern phytopharmacology around sesamin and sesamolin lignans). The seed is one of the oldest cultivated oilseeds, with archaeological domestication at_ Harappan-era Indus Valley (~5500 BCE) _and probable independent domestication in the African Sahel. Status: classical in all four traditions; Qurʾānically attested through the prophetic "open sesame" Aladdin-tradition (Islamic-folk register but not canonical Tibb al-Nabawī)._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Sesamum indicum_ L., family Pedaliaceae. The drug-and-food: dried *seeds* (white / cream — the _Sesami semen album_; black — _Sesami semen nigrum_; multiple intermediate cultivars). Latin _sēsamum_ from Greek σήσαμον (_sḗsamon_), itself a loanword from a Semitic source — Akkadian _šamaššammū_ "sesame; lit. oil-plant" (literally _šamaš_ "sun" + _šammū_ "plant" — possibly "sun-plant," reflecting the seed's solar-association in Mesopotamian ritual). The Akkadian / Akkadian-derived word passed via Aramaic-Phoenician-Hebrew-Arabic into the Mediterranean classical languages, making sesame's name one of the great Bronze-Age Mesopotamian-Mediterranean wanderwörter. The Indus Valley cultivation (~5500 BCE) was independent of the Mesopotamian; the species is one of the *earliest cultivated oilseeds anywhere*.#footnote[Etymology: Lewis & Short (s.v. _sesamum_); Beekes (s.v. σήσαμον — flagged as Semitic loanword); Black, George & Postgate, _A Concise Dictionary of Akkadian_ (s.v. _šamaššammū_). The Mesopotamian-Mediterranean wanderwört trajectory is one of the canonical Bronze-Age oilseed etymologies; the Indus Valley archaeological evidence (Harappan sites at Harappa, Miri Qalat) establishes independent / parallel domestication.]

*English:* _Sesame_, from medieval Latin _sesamum_ via Greek σήσαμον. The phrase *"open sesame"* (from the Aladdin tale in the _Thousand and One Nights_ — a late addition by Antoine Galland's 18th-c. French translation, drawn from Syrian oral tradition) is the most globally recognized cultural-linguistic reference to the seed — though the Galland tale is not in the Arabic Bulaq edition of the _Nights_; the *cultural significance* is real but the *Quranic / canonical hadith provenance is nil*. Flagged: "open sesame" is a *French Orientalist literary creation*, not a classical Islamic register.

*Sanskrit:* *तिल (tila)* — the classical Sanskrit name, used continuously from Vedic times. The Ṛgveda mentions tila; tila is central in Vedic and post-Vedic ritual (the *tarpaṇa* offering — sesame seeds with water — is the canonical ancestor-rite libation, alongside rice and barley); the *piṇḍa-dāna* (rice-ball-and-sesame-water funeral offering) is a *pan-Hindu* funerary tradition that survives essentially unchanged through three millennia. Other Sanskrit names: *जयतिल (jayatila)*, *होमधान्य (homadhānya)* — "fire-grain," for ritual use; *पवित्र (pavitra)* — "purifying"; the seeds carry strong *purificatory* ritual valence. Sesame oil (_tila-taila_) is the *canonical base oil for almost all Ayurvedic medicated oils* (the _taila-pāka-vidhi_ tradition).#footnote[Tila in Vedic ritual: Ṛgveda; Atharvaveda; Brāhmaṇa literature; Manusmṛti and the Dharma-Śāstra register on _tarpaṇa_ and _piṇḍa-dāna_. Charaka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27 — _tila_ in the _śūka-dhānya_ vicinity; Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Dhānya-varga_, _tila_ entry. Tila-taila as base for Ayurvedic medicated oils: Sharangadhara Saṃhitā, _Taila-paka-vidhi_; _Ayurvedic Formulary of India_, Part I.]

*Arabic / Persian / Urdu (Tibb register):* *سِمْسِم (simsim)* — Arabic; one of the oldest classical Arabic plant names; classical Tibb name from Avicenna onward. Persian *کنجد (konjed)*; Urdu *تل (til)* — a Sanskrit borrowing preserved in Indian Muslim register. The Arabic *سِمْسِم* is etymologically related to the Akkadian _šamaššammū_ via the Semitic shared root. Sesame oil in Arabic: *زيت السمسم (zayt al-simsim)*. Sesame paste: *طحينة (ṭaḥīna)* — the foundational Levantine condiment, the base of hummus and many compound preparations. Sesame sweets: *حلاوة طحينية (ḥalāwa ṭaḥīniyya)*, *حلاوة (ḥalāwa)* — the canonical Levantine-Turkish-Maghrebi sweet.#footnote[Simsim in Ibn Sīnā, _al-Qānūn fī al-Ṭibb_, Book II, _simsim_ entry; Ibn al-Bayṭār, _al-Jāmiʿ_, _simsim_ entry; _Khazāyin al-Adwiya_, vol. III. The Arabic plant name is shared across the entire Semitic family.]

*Chinese:* *芝麻 (zhīma)* — the modern Chinese name; *胡麻 (húmá)* — "barbarian hemp / foreign hemp" — the canonical older Chinese name preserved in classical literature, reflecting the herb's *introduction from the Western Regions* (Persia / Central Asia) during the Han dynasty (traditionally attributed to Zhang Qian's missions, 2nd c. BCE, though sesame was likely introduced earlier). The *Shennong Bencao Jing* treats *胡麻 (húmá)* as a *top-grade drug (上品)*, with the seeds, leaves, and oil all having medical use. The black-seeded form *黑芝麻 (hēi zhīma)* / *黑胡麻 (hēi húmá)* is preferred as the medicinal drug (Kidney-yin and Liver-yin tonifying, hair-blackening); the white-seeded form is preferred culinarily. Sesame oil *麻油 (máyóu)* / *香油 (xiāngyóu)* "fragrant oil" is the foundational Chinese cooking oil for cold dressings, dim sum, and aromatic finishing. The Han-dynasty introduction makes sesame a *post-Shennong-but-fully-classical* drug in the Chinese tradition.#footnote[胡麻 / 芝麻 in _Shennong Bencao Jing_ (top grade); _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578 — substantial entry; Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. The 胡 ("barbarian / foreign") naming-marker (also used for 胡椒 huí jiāo black pepper, 胡蘿蔔 húluóbo carrot) reflects the canonical Chinese way of marking Silk-Road introductions.]

*Hebrew:* *שֻׁמְשׁוּם (shumshūm)* — Modern Hebrew, cognate with Arabic _simsim_. Biblical Hebrew does *not* name sesame directly — the canonical biblical attestation is absent, despite Mediterranean cultivation in the Iron Age. Talmudic-rabbinic literature does reference sesame (תַּלְמוּד, _Mishnah_) and uses it widely as food and oil.

=== The paste drug-forms — one seed, three different products

_The ground-sesame pastes of the Levant, China, and Japan are routinely treated as one ingredient and substituted for one another in recipes. They are not interchangeable._ Three processing variables — *whether the hull is removed*, *how far the seed is roasted*, and *how fine the grind* — are set differently in each tradition, and the results differ in colour, texture, flavour, behaviour in water, and mineral content. This is the same _saṃskāra_ principle that governs 生薑/乾薑 ginger and 生地/熟地 rehmannia, operating on a food rather than a drug: *the process makes the substance*.

- *طحينة (ṭaḥīna) / tahini* — Levantine. *Hulled* seed (dehulled by soaking or brining and separating the hull), *lightly* roasted or sometimes raw, ground fine and *loose*: pale ivory, pourable, mild with a clean bitterness. The base of *hummus*, *baba ghanoush*, *tarator* sauce, and *ḥalāwa*. The name is from the Arabic root *ط-ح-ن (ṭ-ḥ-n)*, "to grind" — *طحن (ṭaḥana)* "he ground," *طحين (ṭaḥīn)* "flour" — so it means simply "the ground thing," naming the process and nothing else.
- *芝麻醬 (zhīmajiàng)* — Chinese. *Unhulled* seed (often the brown or black cultivar), roasted *hard*, stone-ground to a thick, stiff, dark, strongly nutty-bitter paste. It is *not* pourable and must be let down before use: water is added a little at a time, whereupon the paste first seizes and stiffens before loosening into a smooth sauce — the technique called *澥 (xiè)*, and a step that surprises anyone who approaches it expecting tahini. Used in 麻醬麵 (sesame-paste noodles), the sesame dip for Beijing lamb hotpot, and 涼拌 cold dressings. *醬 (jiàng)* properly denotes a fermented paste or sauce (soy, bean); sesame paste is *not* fermented, and the character is here used in its looser sense of "thick paste."
- *練り胡麻 (neri goma)* — Japanese, "kneaded sesame"; made white (*白練り胡麻*) or black (*黒練り胡麻*), roasted and ground very smooth. The base of *胡麻だれ (goma-dare)* dressings and *胡麻和え (goma-ae)*, and of *胡麻豆腐 (goma-dōfu)* — sesame "tofu," neri goma set with kudzu starch, a staple of Buddhist temple cuisine (*精進料理, shōjin ryōri*) and one of the few preparations in which sesame paste is the dish rather than a condiment.

*The hull is the consequential variable, and it is nutritional as well as culinary.* The sesame hull carries most of the seed's *calcium* and most of its *oxalate*. Hulled tahini is therefore paler, milder, far lower in oxalate — and *much lower in calcium* than the whole-seed figure usually quoted for sesame. Unhulled pastes (Chinese 芝麻醬, and the "sesame butter" of Western health-food commerce) retain both. *A reader using the per-100 g calcium figure in this entry's composition section should not apply it to tahini.*#footnote[Hulling, roast level and grind as the three variables distinguishing ṭaḥīna, 芝麻醬 and neri goma: standard food-science and regional-culinary literature. The 澥 (xiè) thinning technique for Chinese sesame paste is standard Chinese kitchen practice. Arabic root ط-ح-ن: Lane, _An Arabic-English Lexicon_ (s.v. طحن). The calcium-and-oxalate distribution in the sesame hull, and the consequent divergence between hulled and unhulled paste, is well established in the food-composition literature — verify specific figures against USDA FoodData Central, which lists hulled and unhulled sesame products separately.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

*胡麻 (húmá) / 黑芝麻 (hēi zhīma)* is *top-grade in the Shennong Bencao Jing* and is the *foundational longevity / Kidney-yin-tonifying / hair-blackening drug* of the classical Chinese tradition. The clinical and dietetic uses are well-developed.#footnote[胡麻 / 黑芝麻 in _Shennong Bencao Jing_; elaborated in _Bencao Gangmu_; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.]

*Property (性 xìng / 氣 qì):* Neutral (平); slightly warm in some readings.

*Flavor (味 wèi):* Sweet (甘 gān).

*Channels entered (歸經 guījīng):* Liver (肝), Kidney (腎), Large Intestine (大腸).

*Actions & indications:*
- *補肝腎、益精血 (bǔ gān shèn, yì jīng xuè)* — *tonifies Liver and Kidney, augments essence and blood*; the cardinal action. For *premature graying of the hair, premature aging, dizziness, blurred vision from Liver-Kidney yin vacuity*; for impotence and infertility from Kidney-essence vacuity.
- *烏髮 (wū fā)* — *blackens the hair*. The black sesame variety is taken daily (raw or as 黑芝麻糊 black sesame paste) for graying hair; the classical Chinese use of *black-sesame-for-black-hair* is one of the more famous *signature doctrine* registers (五色 wǔ sè, the five-color correspondences: black foods tonify the Kidney, the organ associated with hair growth and pigment).
- *潤腸通便 (rùn cháng tōng biàn)* — *moistens the intestines, promotes bowel movement* — for constipation from blood-and-fluid vacuity in the elderly; sesame oil and sesame seeds are gentle laxatives appropriate for vacuity patterns.
- *養血 (yǎng xuè)* — nourishes blood — a supporting register.
- *Modern register:* sesame is investigated for its hepatoprotective, antihypertensive, and antioxidant effects; sesamin lignans show LDL-cholesterol-lowering and antioxidant activity.

*Cautions:* damp-heat dysentery; chronic loose stools (the moistening-lubricating action is contraindicated in damp diarrhea patterns); food stagnation. Excessive use is dietetically heavy.

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

*Tila (तिल)* is *centrally classical* — treated by Charaka in Sūtrasthāna 27, by Suśruta, and by Bhāvaprakāśa in the _dhānya-varga_. *Tila-taila (sesame oil)* is the *single most important base substance of Ayurvedic pharmacy*, used as the carrier oil for hundreds of medicated oils (the _taila-pāka-vidhi_ tradition — sesame oil cooked with herbal decoctions and pastes to produce medicated oils like _Mahānārāyaṇa taila_, _Bhṛṅgarāja taila_, _Dhānvantara taila_, etc.). The *black tila* is preferred as the medicinal drug; *white tila* is the canonical dietary; *brown / red tila* for some specific preparations.#footnote[Tila and tila-taila in Charaka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27 and Cikitsāsthāna; Suśruta Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 46; Aṣṭāṅga Hṛdaya, Sūtrasthāna 5; Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Dhānya-varga_, _tila_ entry; Sharangadhara Saṃhitā, _Taila-paka-vidhi_ (the canonical sesame-oil-medicating procedure); _Ayurvedic Formulary of India_, Part I; P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_.]

*Rasa (taste):* Madhura (मधुर, sweet) — predominant; Tikta (तिक्त, bitter — mild); Kaṣāya (कषाय, astringent — mild).

*Guṇa (qualities):* Guru (गुरु, heavy), Snigdha (स्निग्ध, unctuous) — *paradigmatically* (tila and its oil define the unctuous in Indian dietetics), Sūkṣma (सूक्ष्म, subtle / fine — the seed and oil penetrate to deep tissues, a key reason for the medicated-oil register), Vyavāyī (व्यवायी, pervading).

*Vīrya (potency):* Uṣṇa (उष्ण, heating).

*Vipāka (post-digestive taste):* Madhura (मधुर, sweet).#footnote[Rasa–guṇa–vīrya–vipāka of tila: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu; Charaka. The _sūkṣma_ and _vyavāyī_ qualities are the dravyaguna basis for the canonical Ayurvedic preference for sesame oil over coconut or mustard as the base for medicated oils intended for deep-tissue penetration.]

*Prabhāva:* The *strength-and-vitality-promoting* action — particularly the *vājīkaraṇa* (aphrodisiac), *bṛṃhaṇa* (anabolic), and *keśya* (hair-supporting) actions — is *prabhāva-level* in the sense that the actions exceed what the basic rasa-guṇa-vīrya-vipāka would predict. The classical commentators preserve this reading; the modern Ayurvedic literature affirms it. The *daily intake of soaked black sesame in winter* as a *rasāyana* (rejuvenation) practice is preserved across Indian regional Ayurvedic traditions.

*Doṣa effect:* Pacifies *Vāta* strongly (the snigdha-madhura-uṣṇa profile is the canonical Vāta-pacifier); pacifies *Pitta* moderately (the sweet vipāka and ghi-like snigdha); aggravates *Kapha* at heavy intake (the heavy-snigdha register).

*Karma (actions):*
- _Vāta-hara_ — pacifies Vāta; the canonical Vāta-pacifier among grains.
- _Bṛṃhaṇa, Balya_ — bulk-building, strengthening.
- _Vṛṣya_ — aphrodisiac.
- _Keśya_ — hair-supportive (the cross-system convergence with the Chinese *wū fā* / hair-blackening).
- _Tvacya_ — skin-supporting (basis of much Ayurvedic skin and beauty use of til-taila).
- _Sukha-prasava_ — promotes easy childbirth (classical postpartum register).
- _Snehana_ — lubricating; the foundational classical Ayurvedic preparation of the body for purification (_pūrva-karma_ to _pañcakarma_) uses tila-taila as the principal _snehana_ vehicle.
- _Stanyajanana_ — galactagogue.
- _Hṛdya_ — cardiotonic (modest).
- _Used in:_ Vāta disorders broadly, _kārśya_ (emaciation), convalescence, postpartum, _kṣaya_ (wasting), _rasāyana_ regimens, hair / skin disorders, joint disorders, neurological complaints (via medicated oils), _vājīkaraṇa_ (aphrodisiac) regimens.

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S = core saṃhitā, N = later nighaṇṭu, F = folk/regional, M = modern/pharmacological)_

- *(S) Conditional (doṣa / avasthā) viruddha:* heavy tila in Pitta excess, _amlapitta_ (hyperacidity); heavy daily intake in deep Kapha excess and _sthaulya_.
- *(S) Saṃyoga (combination) viruddha:* the classical saṃhitā tradition records that *tila with palāśa-puṣpa (Butea monosperma flowers) and water becomes harmful* — one of the named classical saṃyoga viruddha.
- *(N/F) Combination cautions:* tila preparations with curd / sour milk are folk-cautioned as Kapha-aggravating; tila with strongly pungent or sour foods can compound Pitta.
- *(M) Modern medical:*
  - *Sesame allergy* — sesame is among the rapidly-increasing major food allergens globally; severe anaphylaxis is documented; the FDA in the United States added sesame as the *ninth major food allergen* requiring labeling (effective 2023). *This is a significant modern caveat*.
  - *Anticoagulant effects (mild)* — sesamin has modest antiplatelet activity.
  - *Calcium and oxalate considerations — and the figure does not transfer to every sesame product.* Whole *unhulled* sesame seed is very high in calcium (~1000 mg per 100 g) but also high in oxalate, both being concentrated in the *hull*; roasting and grinding improve bioavailability from the unhulled seed (Chinese 芝麻醬, Western "sesame butter"). *Hulled* products — most notably Levantine *ṭaḥīna* — have had the hull removed and are therefore *much lower in both calcium and oxalate*, so the ~1000 mg figure must not be applied to tahini. See the paste drug-form subsection under Nomenclature for the hulled/unhulled distinction across the three traditions.
  - *Phytic acid* — modest; bioavailability of minerals is affected.#footnote[Sesame allergy is documented in standard allergy literature; the FDA addition as the 9th major allergen reflects rising clinical prevalence. The classical _tila + palāśa-puṣpa_ saṃyoga viruddha is preserved in Charaka. Verify modern medical caveats against current allergy and pharmacology guidelines.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

*Simsim (سِمْسِم)* is *fully classical in Tibb* — Avicenna treats it in _al-Qānūn_ Book II as a substantial drug; Ibn al-Bayṭār treats it; the Levantine, Maghrebi, and Persian-Mughal culinary traditions preserve continuous use of the seed, oil, paste, and sweets. The Tibb register on simsim is *nutritive-warming-deobstruent*. See `tibb-al-arabi.typ` for the framework.

*Arabic:* سِمْسِم (_simsim_); Persian: کنجد (_konjed_); Urdu: تل (_til_), سمسم. Sesame oil: زيت السمسم (_zayt al-simsim_); sesame paste: طحينة (_ṭaḥīna_); sesame sweet: حلاوة / حلوى (_ḥalāwa / ḥalwā_).

*Mizāj:* *Hot in the 1st degree, Moist in the 1st degree* per Avicenna (_al-Qānūn_ II, _simsim_) — a mild warming-moistening placement reflecting the nutritive-snigdha character; some sources mark the dryness slightly differently for the seed vs. oil.

*Afʿāl:*
- *Mughadhdhī ghidhāʾan kaẓīran* — substantially nutritive.
- *Muqawwī al-badan* — body-strengthening.
- *Yuqawwī al-bāh* — supports sexual potency (the cross-system convergent _vājīkaraṇa_ register).
- *Mulayyin* — mildly laxative.
- *Muḥallil* — resolvent (for cold-pattern obstructions).
- *Mufattiḥ li-l-suddad* — mild deobstruent.
- *Munaḍḍif li-l-ṣadr* — chest-cleansing (for cold-pattern phlegm).
- *Tvacy / skin-and-hair register:* extensive Tibb use of simsim oil for hair-strengthening, scalp-massage, skin-emollient — the cross-system convergence with Ayurvedic _keśya_ and Chinese 烏髮 is striking.
- *Mughadhdhī li-l-ḥāmil wa-l-murḍiʿ* — nutritive for pregnant and lactating women; sesame is the canonical Levantine postpartum food alongside fenugreek.

*Istemālāt:* convalescence; weakness; emaciation; postpartum recovery; sexual debility; hair loss and graying; dry skin; cold-pattern chest conditions; constipation; daily nutritive use; as base oil for compound aromatic oils.

*Mawāneʿ / Iḥtirāzāt:* hot-mizāj patients at excessive intake; rancid oil should be avoided; sesame allergy (modern caveat).

*Muṣliḥ:* honey; rose-water; almonds — for mild balance; vinegar for the oil in some compound preparations.

*Badal:* almonds (_lawz_); pistachios (_fustuq_); walnuts (_jawz_) — for the nutritive-oil register; flax (_kattān_) for the seed-oil register.

*Classical Tibb formulas:*
- *Ṭaḥīna (طحينة) — sesame paste*. The canonical Levantine ingredient: ground roasted sesame seeds, smooth and oily; used as a base in countless compound preparations, both medical and culinary. *Hummus* (chickpea-and-tahini paste) and *baba ghanoush* (eggplant-and-tahini) are global Levantine staples.
- *Ḥalāwa / ḥalwā (حلاوة / حلوى) — sesame sweet*. The canonical Levantine-Turkish-Maghrebi sweet: ṭaḥīna with sugar or honey, sometimes with pistachios, vanilla, or chocolate; eaten as confection and as nutritive food-medicine. *Persian sohan halva* is a related sweet. The *Indian Muslim halva* register includes sesame-based preparations.
- *Sesame-and-honey paste* for cough and bronchial conditions in Maghrebi and Levantine folk medicine.
- *Roasted-sesame-and-jaggery sweets* in the Indian-Muslim register, paralleling the Hindu til-laḍḍū.
- *Sesame oil as base for compound aromatic oils* (e.g. infused with herbs like marzanjūsh, rose, gum mastic) — for hair, scalp, joint pain.

*Ṭibb al-Nabawī attestation:* *No direct Qurʾānic attestation.* The famous "open sesame" phrase from the Aladdin / Ali Baba tale is a *late literary creation* (Antoine Galland's 18th-c. French translation of Syrian oral tradition, not in the Arabic Bulaq edition of the _Thousand and One Nights_) and is *not* a Quranic or hadith reference. Some hadith narrations include sesame in dietary contexts (the Prophet's table is recorded as including various oils and seeds), but no specific canonical hadith elevates sesame to the prophetic-medicine status that black seed (_ḥabbat al-baraka_) or dates have. The herb's classical Tibb status is *Avicennan / Galenic-Arabic*, with *strong cultural Islamic register* but modest specific hadith attestation. Honest scholarly register: *the cultural register is strong, the canonical Tibb register is strong, the Qurʾānic / Tibb al-Nabawī register is mild but present in some narrations*.#footnote[Ibn Sīnā, _al-Qānūn fī al-Ṭibb_, Book II, _simsim_; Ibn al-Bayṭār, _al-Jāmiʿ_, _simsim_; _Khazāyin al-Adwiya_, vol. III. The "open sesame" Galland-French literary origin: standard scholarship on the _Thousand and One Nights_ orientalist editions. Some sesame hadith narrations are documented but not at the prophetic-medicine canonical level.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

- *黑芝麻丸 / 黑芝麻糊 (Hēi Zhīma Wán / Hú, Black Sesame Pill / Paste)* — the daily black sesame paste, often with honey and/or rice; for premature graying, blurred vision, dryness, constipation in the elderly. The canonical Chinese longevity preparation.
- *桑麻丸 (Sāng Má Wán, Mulberry-Sesame Pill)* — black sesame + mulberry leaf; for Liver-yin vacuity with dizziness and graying.
- *麻仁丸 (Má Rén Wán, Hemp Seed Pill)* — for elderly constipation; sesame seeds are sometimes substituted in modern formulary; the original is hemp seed (火麻仁) but sesame is in the same register.
- *Sesame oil (麻油 máyóu / 香油 xiāngyóu)* — extensive culinary and external-medical use; topical for skin conditions, sesame-oil-and-ginger compresses for cold-pattern abdominal pain.
- *Daily dietetic preparation:* black sesame congee, black sesame cookies, sesame oil dressings, sesame-paste noodles (麻醬麵 málàmiàn).#footnote[Formula sources: Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; modern Chinese dietetics literature.]

=== Ayurveda

- *Tila-taila (तिल तैल)* — the *foundational base oil of Ayurvedic pharmacy*. Used as the carrier for hundreds of medicated oils (taila): *Mahānārāyaṇa taila*, *Dhānvantara taila*, *Bhṛṅgarāja taila*, *Aśvagandha-bala-lakṣādī taila*, *Bala-aśvagandha taila*, *Karpūrādi taila*, *Triphalādya taila*, *Kṣīrabalā taila*, *Nārāyaṇa taila*, *Pinda taila*, *Mahāmāṣa taila* — and many more. The *taila-pāka-vidhi* procedure (cooking sesame oil with herbal decoctions, pastes, and milk according to detailed classical recipes) is one of the most elaborate procedures in Ayurvedic pharmacy.
- *Abhyaṅga (अभ्यङ्ग)* — the *daily oil massage* — canonically uses tila-taila as the preferred oil. Listed in Charaka as a daily morning practice (_dinacaryā_) for general health and longevity.
- *Snehana for pañcakarma* — sesame oil as the primary internal and external oleation vehicle for the preparatory phase of classical purification.
- *Til-laḍḍū / til-pīṭhā / til-gud preparations* — winter sweet preparations: roasted sesame + jaggery (sometimes with peanuts, cardamom, dry ginger); eaten on *Makara Saṅkrānti* (the winter solstice / sun-northing festival, January 14–15 in modern reckoning), as the canonical winter warming-nourishing sweet. The sesame-jaggery combination is climatically and constitutionally precise for the cold-dry Indian winter.
- *Tila in Hindu ritual (the deepest ritual register of any food in the Indian tradition):*
  - *Tarpaṇa (तर्पण)* — the daily ancestor-libation: sesame seeds with water poured to the ancestors. *Performed daily* by traditional Hindu families.
  - *Piṇḍa-dāna (पिण्ड दान)* — the funeral and post-funeral offering of rice balls with sesame water. Foundational Hindu funerary ritual; performed at Gayā, Kāśī, and other sacred sites for liberation of ancestors.
  - *Tila-snāna* (sesame-oil bath) — the ritual bath with sesame oil on auspicious days.
  - *Tila-dāna* — gifting sesame seeds as a meritorious act, especially during eclipses, solstices, and the death-anniversary śrāddha rites.
  The ritual valence of tila as *the canonical funerary and ancestor food* — sustained across three millennia of Hindu practice — is *unmatched* by any other plant in the Indian materia medica.
- *Compound Ayurvedic preparations:* *Aśvagandhādi cūrṇa*, *Karpūrādi cūrṇa* — tila as supporting ingredient; *Tila-kalpa* and *til-arka* preparations.#footnote[Tila in classical Ayurvedic formulary: Sharangadhara Saṃhitā, Madhyama-khaṇḍa, _Taila-paka-vidhi_; Bhaiṣajya-ratnāvalī; _Ayurvedic Formulary of India_; classical Vedic and Dharma-Śāstra literature on tarpaṇa and piṇḍa-dāna. The Hindu ritual register of tila is sustained from Ṛgvedic times to the present.]

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

- *Greco-Roman:* sesame (σήσαμον _sḗsamon_, _sesamum_) was a known and cultivated food in the eastern Mediterranean from the Bronze Age. Dioscorides (_De Materia Medica_ II.121) treats sesame as nutritive, mildly laxative, hot in the second degree, with the seed and the oil both having use. Pliny (_Naturalis Historia_ XV) gives cultivation and culinary uses. Galen treats sesame as warming, nourishing, with caveats on heavy consumption causing biliousness in some constitutions.
- *Hellenistic-Byzantine confectionery:* sesame-and-honey sweets (the ancient ancestor of modern halva) are documented in classical Greek sources.
- *Medieval European:* sesame appears in Mediterranean monastic and Salernitan pharmacy as a nutritive seed and emollient oil; the Levantine ṭaḥīna and ḥalāwa traditions were preserved through medieval Iberian (Andalusian) and Sicilian Arab influence.
- *Modern Western herbal:* sesame oil and tahini are widely used in modern dietetics; sesame is recognized as a nutrient-dense food; modern phytotherapy uses *sesamin extract* for cholesterol management and antioxidant supplementation.#footnote[Greco-Roman sources: Dioscorides, _De Materia Medica_ II.121 — Beck translation; Pliny, _Naturalis Historia_ XV (Loeb / Rackham); Galen, _On the Properties of Foodstuffs_. Medieval and modern: standard pharmacy and dietetic literature.]

=== Ṭibb (Arabic-Islamic medicine)

Covered above. The Tibb formulary use of simsim is extensively elaborated — ṭaḥīna, ḥalāwa, sesame oil as base for compound preparations, and as canonical postpartum and convalescent nutritive food.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

Sesame is a *warm-to-hot, dry-season-tolerant* annual — adapted to *warm-dry monsoon-fringe, warm-Mediterranean, hot-arid subtropical, and hot-tropical* climates. The plant tolerates considerable heat and modest drought once established; it is *not* a temperate-cool-season crop. Modern cultivation is dominated by India, Sudan, Myanmar, Tanzania, Nigeria, China, and Ethiopia. See `climates.typ` for the foundational framework.

=== Hot-arid and warm-dry (Levant, Maghreb, Middle East — the Tibb / Mediterranean register)

The classical Tibb / Levantine / Mediterranean climate. The seed-and-oil-and-paste register is climatically apt: nutritive-snigdha sesame oil and ṭaḥīna pair with cooling-sour adjuvants (lemon, vinegar, sumac, yogurt) in the canonical hummus / tahini-and-pita / halva combinations. The summer-cooling, winter-warming dual register is preserved through the seasonal cooking patterns.

=== Hot-humid tropical / monsoonal (South Asia — the Ayurvedic register)

The classical Ayurvedic _ānūpa_-fringe climate. Sesame is grown across India in both the kharif and rabi (winter) seasons; the *winter til-laḍḍū / til-jaggery sweets at Makara Saṅkrānti* are the climatically apt classical preparation — warm-moistening-snigdha for the brief Indian winter; the *summer use is moderated*.

=== Continental seasonal-extreme (northern China — post-Han classical introduction)

The Chinese classical register absorbed sesame from the Silk Road and integrated it into the *winter-tonic* preparations: 黑芝麻糊 black sesame paste is a canonical winter Spleen-Kidney-yin-tonifying preparation.

=== Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_)

Modern globalized health-food culture has spread sesame across all climates. The Hindu ritual register has spread across the Indian diaspora; the Levantine ṭaḥīna register has spread globally with the hummus boom. *The classical constitutional register* — *warm-pacifying-snigdha-vāta-pacifying* — is broadly applicable, with summer / Pitta-excess use moderated through compound pairings. See Caraka Sū. 26 for the _viruddha_ framework.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Approximate composition (whole sesame seed, per 100 g):*
- Energy ~573 kcal; Water ~5 g
- Carbohydrate ~23 g (fiber ~12 g)
- Protein ~18 g (with all essential amino acids; high methionine — making sesame a complementary protein to legumes)
- Fat ~50 g (predominantly *linoleic acid* — polyunsaturated; oleic acid — monounsaturated; small amount of saturated)
- Minerals: *calcium ~975 mg* (very high — among the highest plant calcium sources), magnesium ~351 mg, iron ~14.5 mg, zinc ~7.8 mg, potassium ~468 mg, phosphorus very high
- Vitamins: vitamin E (modest), B-vitamins (thiamin, niacin, B6, folate)
- *Sesamin and sesamolin* — *the headline lignans*; dibenzylbutyrolactone-class compounds with documented hepatoprotective, antioxidant, hypocholesterolemic activity; *sesame is one of the richest dietary sources of lignans*
- *Sesamol* — phenolic antioxidant generated from sesamolin on roasting; contributes to oil stability
- *Phytosterols* — among the highest in plant foods; cholesterol-lowering register
- *Tocopherols* — γ-tocopherol particularly; antioxidant
- *Oxalate* (~340 mg per 100 g whole, lower in hulled): mineral-bioavailability consideration#footnote[Phytochemistry and nutrition: standard nutritional databases (USDA FoodData Central); recent reviews in _Phytochemistry_, _Journal of Functional Foods_, _Nutrients_; sesamin and sesamolin literature.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Cholesterol-lowering* — sesame consumption (~25–50 g per day of seeds) and standardized sesamin/sesamolin supplementation reduce LDL cholesterol; supported by multiple RCTs and meta-analyses.
- *Antihypertensive* — modest blood-pressure-lowering effect from sesame consumption and sesame oil; supported by RCTs in pre-hypertensive populations.
- *Antioxidant and hepatoprotective* — sesamin and sesamolin show robust hepatoprotective activity in preclinical models; clinical use as supportive herb in chronic liver disease.
- *Bone health* — high calcium and magnesium; tahini and sesame paste are major contributors to calcium intake in plant-forward and vegan diets.
- *Anti-inflammatory* — preclinical and modest clinical.
- *Sesame allergy* — *significant and rising clinical prevalence*; FDA-listed as 9th major food allergen (US, 2023); severe IgE-mediated reactions documented.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Dietetic register:* unrestricted at normal culinary intake (2 tbsp / 30 g of seeds, oil, or paste per day is typical and well-tolerated).
- *Therapeutic register:* 25–50 g of seeds per day for cholesterol-lowering effect; standardized sesamin extracts 50–100 mg per day.
- *Tila-taila Abhyaṅga:* daily oil massage uses 20–100 mL of sesame oil externally; well-tolerated.
- *Pregnancy:* safe at dietary intake; the strong _sukha-prasava_ classical reputation supports use in late pregnancy.
- *Sesame allergy:* avoid; cross-reactivity with other seeds is variable.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*#footnote[Dose ranges: standard nutrition and supplement literature; cholesterol-lowering RCTs; verify against current allergy guidelines for FDA major-allergen status.]

